386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 74 0 R
/T (Forward path. A packet from the instance in VPC A to a 10.20.x.x address matches the 10.20.0.0/16 route in ewb-rt-a-private, whose target is the peering connection. It crosses the peering link and arrives in VPC B, where the destination security group must allow the traffic.)
/E (Forward path. A packet from the instance in VPC A to a 10.20.x.x address matches the 10.20.0.0/16 route in ewb-rt-a-private, whose target is the peering connection. It crosses the peering link and arrives in VPC B, where the destination security group must allow the traffic.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 74 0 R
/T (Return path. The reply to a 10.10.x.x address matches the 10.10.0.0/16 route in ewb-rt-b-private, which also points at the peering connection. Without that route the reply would have nowhere to go, so a one-way route is not enough. The security group is stateful, so the reply needs no extra inbound rule.)
/E (Return path. The reply to a 10.10.x.x address matches the 10.10.0.0/16 route in ewb-rt-b-private, which also points at the peering connection. Without that route the reply would have nowhere to go, so a one-way route is not enough. The security group is stateful, so the reply needs no extra inbound rule.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
