427 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 75 0 R
/T (This project showed me that a working network is the combination of several small pieces, and that each piece has one clear job. A VPC peering connection only creates the link. Routes on both sides send the traffic across it, and security groups decide which hosts may talk. I also learned why careful CIDR planning at the start matters, since overlapping ranges cannot be fixed later without rebuilding. Testing from both directions, and breaking one rule on purpose, made it much easier to understand where a connectivity problem can come from. The same ideas apply to many real AWS designs, such as connecting application and database environments privately.)
/E (This project showed me that a working network is the combination of several small pieces, and that each piece has one clear job. A VPC peering connection only creates the link. Routes on both sides send the traffic across it, and security groups decide which hosts may talk. I also learned why careful CIDR planning at the start matters, since overlapping ranges cannot be fixed later without rebuilding. Testing from both directions, and breaking one rule on purpose, made it much easier to understand where a connectivity problem can come from. The same ideas apply to many real AWS designs, such as connecting application and database environments privately.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
